<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Eastern Guo</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 767 BCE (approx.) · capital Zhi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Ji-clan state near Xingyang founded by a brother of King Wen of Zhou and swallowed by the newly arrived State of Zheng in 767 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-state">Guo</a> was established early in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou</a> by Guo Shu or Guo Zhong, younger brothers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen</a>, whose descendants held two separate Guo states. The eastern one, centred at Zhi near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xingyang">Xingyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, guarded the approaches to the eastern capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers were close kin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> kings, but little else is known of their government. The state controlled strategic ground near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xingyang">Xingyang</a> pass.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> court moved east in 770 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-zheng">Duke Huan of Zheng</a> had already deposited his family and treasure with Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-state">Guo</a> and nearby Kuai. His son Duke Wu of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-zheng">Zheng</a> conquered Eastern Guo in 767 BCE, and the State of Zheng built its power on its lands.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eastern-guo.jpg" alt="Eastern Guo" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 767 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zhi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guo (Zhou state)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-906&amp;focus=eastern-guo" aria-label="Eastern Guo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7503 919 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eastern Guo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7823 1175L7789 1186"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7789 1186h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7823 1175h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7823 1175h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-906&amp;focus=eastern-guo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

